"Completely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.09698)\nciteturn11academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Title: Completely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph ... The existence of a CR code in such Cayley graph $G$ implies the existence of a CR code with the same parameters in the corresponding Hamming graph that covers $G$.\nTitle: Completely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph\nAuthors: Sergey Goryainov, Denis Krotov\nDate: Thu Nov 14 18:59:01 2024\n\nIn Cayley graphs on the additive group of a small vector space over GF$(q)$, $q=2,3$, we look for completely regular (CR) codes whose parameters are new in Hamming graphs over the same field. The existence of a CR code in such Cayley graph $G$ implies the existence of a CR code with the same parameters in the corresponding Hamming graph that covers $G$. In such a way, we find several completely regular codes with new parameters in Hamming graphs over GF$(3)$. The most interesting findings are two new CR-$1$ (with covering radius~$1$) codes that are independent sets (such CR are equivalent to optimal orthogonal arrays attaining the Bierbrauer--Friedman bound) and one new CR-$2$. By recursive constructions, every knew CR code induces an infinite sequence of CR codes (in particular, optimal orthogonal arrays if the original code was CR-$1$ and independent). In between, we classify feasible parameters of CR codes in several strongly regular graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompletely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2411.09698v1)\nciteturn11search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Completely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph ... # • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... The existence of a CR code in such Cayley graph $G$ implies the existence of a CR code with the same parameters in the corresponding Hamming graph that covers $G$.\n\n## Completely regular codes in graphs covered by a Hamming graph\n\nSergey Goryainov, Denis Krotov\n\nPublished: 2024-11-14\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ngraph group regular graph sequence strongly regular graph\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn Cayley graphs on the additive group of a small vector space over GF$(q)$, $q=2,3$, we look for completely regular (CR) codes whose parameters are new in Hamming graphs over the same field. The existence of a CR code in such Cayley graph $G$ implies the existence of a CR code with the same parameters in the corresponding Hamming graph that covers $G$. In such a way, we find several completely regular codes with new parameters in Hamming graphs over GF$(3)$. The most interesting findings are two new CR-$1$ (with covering radius~$1$) codes that are independent sets (such CR are equivalent to optimal orthogonal arrays attaining the Bierbrauer--Friedman bound) and one new CR-$2$. By recursive constructions, every knew CR code induces an infinite sequence of CR codes (in particular, optimal orthogonal arrays if the original code was CR-$1$ and independent). In between, we classify feasible parameters of CR codes in several strongly regular graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA generalization of parking functions (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v27i1p33/pdf/)\nciteturn11search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Rearrangements of k-Naples parking functions ... Characterizing when a rearrangement of a k-Naples parking function is another k- ... If there is only one corner above the line y = n −x then that parking\nNow c5 ﬁnds its preferred space and the three\npreceding occupied. Additionally, when it checks forward, there are no available spaces\nand c5 cannot park. Thus, β /∈PF7,3.\nCharacterizing when a rearrangement of a k-Naples parking function is another k-\nNaples parking function remains an open problem. We state this formally below.\nProblem E. Characterize and enumerate which rearrangements of decreasing k-Naples\nparking functions are also elements of PFn,k.\nIn the case where k = 1 we conjecture the following.\nConjecture 5.6. If there is only one corner above the line y = n −x then that parking\npreference and all of its rearrangements are elements of PFn,1.\nAnother problem of interest would be to consider the case where there are m cars\nin a parking lot with n spaces, where m < n, and cars are allowed to back up k spots.\nThis idea leads naturally to asking about the enumeration of k-Naples (m, n)-parking\nfunctions, where the k = 0 case is enumerated by (n+1−m)(n+1)m−1 [5, Lemma 2]. We\nend by pointing the interested reader to the work of Carlson, Christensen, Harris, Jones,\nand Ramos Rodr´ıguez, which provides many new directions for further investigation on\ngeneralized parking functions [2].\nAcknowledgements\nThe authors thank Alyson Baumgardner and Katie Johnson for introducing us to the\nNaples parking function problem. We also thank Ayomikun Adeniran for helpful conver-\nsations at the beginning stages of this project.\nthe electronic journal of combinatorics 27(1) (2020), #P1.33\n17--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRamsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/344331/ramsey-robustness-conjecture-for-ramsey-infinite-graphs)\nciteturn11search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Ramsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs ... Ramsey robustness conjecture.\n\nRamsey robustness conjecture for Ramsey-infinite graphs\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $r\\geq 2$r≥2, let $H$H be a graph, and let $\\mathcal{M}_r(H)$M r​(H) denote the family of $r$r-Ramsey-minimal graphs for $H$H. Let $\\beta_r(H;G)$β r​(H;G) be the Ramsey robustness of $G$G with respect to $H$H. A graph $H$H is $r$r-Ramsey-i